          #piAs2Functor.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The operation of forming
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
              is equivalently the operation of forming the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+product"} homotopy fiber product
              of the point inclusion with itself, and hence extends to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-functor"} (2,1)-functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Grp
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\pi_1 \colon Grpd^{\ast/} \longrightarrow Grp
                    \\,.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Restricted to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} connected groupoids
              among the pointed groupoids, the functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Grp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1 \colon Grpd^{\ast/}_{\geq 1} \longrightarrow Grp
              of remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#piAs2Functor"}
              is an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%282%2C1%29-categories"} equivalence of (2,1)-categories
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              It is clear that the functor is essentially surjective: for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
              then
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi G
                      %mo ,
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(\mathbf{B}G,\ast) \simeq G
            %p
              The more interesting point to notice is that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
              is indeed a fully faithful
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-functor"} (2,1)-functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,x), (Y,y) \in Grpd^{\ast/}_{\geq 1}
              then the functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Y
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Grp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\pi_1)_{X,Y}
                    \\colon
                    Grpd^{\ast/}((X,y),(Y,y))
                    \\longrightarrow
                    Grp(\pi_1(X,x), \pi_1(Y,y))
            %p
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+groupoids"} equivalence
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-groupoids"} hom-groupoids
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SkeletalRepresentativesForConnectedGroupoids"}
              it is sufficient to check this for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \mathbf{B}G
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y = \mathbf{B}H
              with their canonical basepoints, hence to check that for any two groups
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G,H
              the functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi H
                    %mo ,
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Grp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\pi_1)_{X,Y}
                    \\;\colon\;
                    Grpd^{\ast/}((\mathbf{B}G,\ast),(\mathbf{B}H,\ast))
                    \\longrightarrow
                    Grp(G,H)
            %p is an equivalence.
            %p
              To see this, observe that, by definition of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+objects"} pointed objects
              via the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/undercategory"} undercategory
              under the point, a morphism in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grpd^{\ast/}
              between groupoids of this form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}(-)
              is a diagram in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Grp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grp
              (unpointed) of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ⇙
                              %mi h
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi G
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                  %mi B
                                %mi ϕ
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; \ast
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \swarrow &amp;\swArrow_{h}&amp; \searrow
                    \\\
                    \\mathbf{B}G
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\underset{\mathbf{B}\phi}{\longrightarrow}
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\mathbf{B}H
                    }
            %p
              where the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
              is equivalently just the choice of an element
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \in H
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi,h)
              of a group homomorphism and an element of the domain.
            %p
              We claim that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-functor"} (2,1)-functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
              takes such
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi,h)
              to the homomorphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
